WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.580
(upbeat music)

00:00:06.980 --> 00:00:09.520
The world around us is constantly changing,

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:09.984
and we do our best

00:00:09.984 --> 00:00:14.784
to adapt to different conditions. If it's sunny outside, wearing sunglasses

00:00:14.784 --> 00:00:19.344
is a good choice, but if it's raining, you'll need to put on a raincoat.

00:00:19.344 --> 00:00:19.968
Just like humans,

00:00:19.968 --> 00:00:23.648
computer programs also change depending on different conditions.

00:00:23.648 --> 00:00:27.888
To make our programs responsive, we use conditional statements.

00:00:27.888 --> 00:00:29.953
Conditional statements are commands that

00:00:29.953 --> 00:00:34.673
run a specific block of code only if something is true.

00:00:34.673 --> 00:00:37.753
The simplest conditional is an if statement.

00:00:37.753 --> 00:00:39.937
An if statement uses a Boolean

00:00:39.937 --> 00:00:42.977
expression to ask a true or false question,

00:00:42.977 --> 00:00:46.017
and then runs the code inside if the Boolean evaluates

00:00:46.017 --> 00:00:49.057
to true, like this.

00:00:49.057 --> 00:00:49.922
Let's look at an example

00:00:49.922 --> 00:00:51.922
of conditionals in action.

00:00:53.922 --> 00:00:55.922
First, we'll create a sprite.

00:00:56.922 --> 00:00:59.907
Then we'll use the draw function to draw the

00:00:59.907 --> 00:01:09.891
background, and move the sprite across the screen using the counter pattern.

00:01:09.891 --> 00:01:12.471
(upbeat music)

00:01:19.876 --> 00:01:27.656
We'll add a conditional if statement inside the draw function.

00:01:27.656 --> 00:01:29.856
Inside the conditional, we'll put a Boolean expression

00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:33.860
that asks, "Is the sprite's x position greater than 200?"

00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:37.860
In this case, let's change the look of the sprite

00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:39.845
if it moves past the middle of the screen.

00:01:39.845 --> 00:01:44.845
Finally, we'll draw our sprite and see what happens.

00:01:49.829 --> 00:01:58.829
Computers only do exactly what we tell them to do, but with conditionals, we can have our programs adapt to changes and respond to user input.

00:01:59.814 --> 00:02:02.394
(upbeat music)
